"""Frozen, hand-editable PV day — the values every static register serves.

A literal table of register values at five-minute resolution, keyed by
``"HH:MM"`` and then by **register address**. 288 rows covering one whole day.

    PV_PROFILE_STATIC.rows["13:00"][4]  ->  2604

This is the ONLY source of values for ``type="profile_static"`` registers.
Nothing is computed at read time: ``MockRegisterBlock`` turns the clock into
a row key and indexes straight into this table. Edit a number here and that is
exactly what the register reports at that time.

Keyed by address rather than name so a row stays valid if a register is renamed,
and so it reads like the register map a client actually talks to. Lookups snap
down to the nearest slot; no interpolation. ``PROFILE_DAY_MINUTES`` compresses
the day, so a low value walks this table quickly.

Because each address is an independent column, the table expresses things a
single shared curve could not: ambient temperature (1008) peaks at 15:00 rather
than at solar noon because air lags the sun, equipment temperatures (5, 1009)
blend irradiance with ambient, and the power/current registers are derated by
module temperature so they top out below their ceilings on a hot afternoon.

Covers device_1's static addresses — 14 columns (holding 1, 2, 4–8, 10; input
1004–1009), of which `1` is a leftover no register reads. A register declared
``type="profile_static"`` whose address is missing here fails at startup rather
than quietly serving a default.

Wrapped in :class:`~app.models.StaticProfile`, a plain pydantic model of two
typed fields, so slot keys must be real ``HH:MM`` times and addresses/values
must be in-range integers. The lookup and the "every row supplies this address"
check live in ``app/datastore.py``.

Seeded from the sun curve in ``pv_day_generator.py``, but it is plain data
now — edit it freely.
"""
